<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch06_P04_Ex5_smallest_constant</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>(a) What is the least value of \(\sin x\cos x + 1\)?</p>
<div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</div>
<div>[[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p>(b) For which values of \(c\) are the graphs of \(y = \lvert {@aa@}\sin x\cos x + c\rvert\) and \(y = {@aa@}\sin x\cos x + c\) identical? Enter the smallest such value of \(c\).</p>
<div>\(c =\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</div>
<div>[[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>\(\lvert t\rvert = t\) exactly when \(t \ge 0\), so the graphs of \(y = \lvert f(x)\rvert\) and \(y = f(x)\) are identical exactly when \(f(x) \ge 0\) for every \(x\).</p>
<p>(a) By the double angle formula, \(\sin x\cos x = \dfrac12\sin 2x\), and \(-1 \le \sin 2x \le 1\). So \(\sin x\cos x + 1\) lies between \(\dfrac12\) and \(\dfrac32\). Its least value is \(\dfrac12\), at \(x = \dfrac{3\pi}{4}\) for example. In particular it is never negative, and this proves the exercise.</p>
<p>(b) In the same way \(\displaystyle {@aa@}\sin x\cos x = {@aa/2@}\sin 2x\), so \({@aa@}\sin x\cos x + c\) has least value \(\displaystyle c - {@aa/2@}\), which is at least 0 exactly when \(\displaystyle c \ge {@aa/2@}\). The smallest such value is \(\displaystyle c = {@ta2@}\). For a smaller \(c\) the expression is negative near \(x = \dfrac{3\pi}{4}\), and there the modulus reflects the graph in the \(x\)-axis.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1000000</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
