    <node><name>1</name><description>Diagnostic: sin x cos x taken to reach -1</description><answertest>AlgEquiv</answertest><sans>ans1</sans><tans>0</tans><testoptions></testoptions><quiet>0</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>0.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt1-2-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>\(\sin x\cos x\) never reaches \(-1\): that would need one of \(\sin x\) and \(\cos x\) to be 1 and the other \(-1\) at the same \(x\), and when one of them is \(\pm 1\) the other is 0. Use \(\sin 2x = 2\sin x\cos x\) to find the least value of \(\sin x\cos x\).</p>]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>-1</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt1-2-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Use \(\sin 2x = 2\sin x\cos x\) to write \(\sin x\cos x\) in terms of \(\sin 2x\), whose least value you know.</p>]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
